// # AArch64 cross-architecture equivalence audit (Phase 7 of the 9-phase 11/10 plan)
//
// Logline: Records the cross-architecture equivalence probe that identified the aarch64 int-write path is correct and the x86 path has a cvttsd2si regression that blocks byte-for-byte equivalence.
//
// Setup: Re-reads fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo and the host x86 + aarch64 pipelines.
//
// Beats:
//   1. The probe source is a single println(12345) main.
//   2. AArch64 path: emit -> as -> ld -> qemu-aarch64 prints "12345", exit 0.
//   3. x86 path: emit -> as fails on cvttsd2si / subsd / mulsd / addsd operand type mismatch at _println_int. The function body is generated unconditionally and includes double-arithmetic instructions even for an Int input.

== AArch64 pipeline (works) ==

* Source: fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo
* Emit: ./oodac/oodac.aarch64 emit-aarch64 -> /tmp/e_arm.s
* Assembler: aarch64-linux-gnu-as -> /tmp/e_arm.o
* Linker: aarch64-linux-gnu-ld -> /tmp/e_arm
* Run: qemu-aarch64 /tmp/e_arm -> "12345", exit 0

== x86 pipeline (regressed) ==

* Source: fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo
* Emit: ./oodac/oodac emit-x86 -> /tmp/e_x86.s
* Assembler: as -> error
* Errors: cvttsd2si, subsd, mulsd, addsd operand type mismatch at _println_int

The x86 _println_int is generated unconditionally with both Int (e.g. `cvttsd2si %xmm0, %rax`) and double (`subsd`, `mulsd`, `addsd`) instructions. The operand types are mismatched: `cvttsd2si` expects an XMM register and a 32/64-bit GPR, but the generated code uses mismatched types. This is a regression in x86_emit_int.oo that is independent of the AArch64 work.

== Tag and version ==

Tag v0.225.36 on commit (Phase 7 commit). ooda.pkg version=0.225.36.

== Remaining gap ==

* Phase 8 (pattern matching and closures fixtures): port more source patterns.
* Phase 9 (Tier 1 wiring): unify aarch64_main.oo to also call the AST path; add a general user-function codegen.
* The x86 _println_int regression is a separate piece of work.
